{"situation": "OpCo's support chatbot showed a customer another subscriber's billing address and recent call records (S2-I1); the account-lookup tool was switched off and then restored scoped to the caller's own account, and the twin now reports leak_count 0 with benign_success_rate 1.0 (S2-FB01, S2-FB04). I do not know how many subscribers' records were exposed, over what period, who the affected data subjects are, or whether and by when OpCo must notify the regulator or those data subjects.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "S2-I1 reports that the support chatbot displayed another subscriber's billing address and recent call records to a customer.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 7(1) requires the Controller to take technical and organisational measures so Personal Data is not breached, destroyed, altered or tampered with.",
        "inference": "Chatbot query and session logs, the account-lookup tool configuration and the S2-FB01/S2-FB04 metric snapshots must be frozen now so the exposure can be scoped and the evidence cannot be lost."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0021", "quote": "Take appropriate technical and organizational measures to implement the necessary standards to protect and secure Personal Data in order to preserve its confidentiality and privacy, and to ensure that it is not breached, destroyed, altered or tampered with"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {"to": "CSC", "content": "Retain the customer's original message and the screenshot showing another subscriber's billing address and recent call records, treat any further chatbot data-exposure reports the same way, and confirm which subscriber's records were displayed. Do not delete or edit the material; it is incident evidence."},
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The only evidence of the cross-account disclosure is a screenshot posted by a customer and handled by customer care (S2-I1).",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 7(1) requires measures that keep Personal Data from being breached, altered or tampered with, and the Controller must be able to account for the processing it runs.",
        "inference": "Customer care must preserve and hand over that material before it is deleted or a retention timer expires, and must identify the affected subscriber so the exposure can be scoped."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0032", "quote": "Protect and secure data processing, the electronic media and devices used in processing and the Personal Data they contain"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1", "S2-FB01", "S2-FB04"],
      "statement": "Incident report from NOC: a customer's screenshot (S2-I1) shows OpCo's support chatbot displaying another subscriber's billing address and recent call records. Containment so far: disable_tool was executed, then restore_scope was executed with scope own_account (S2-FB01, S2-FB04). Current twin metrics: leak_count 0, benign_success_rate 1.0; S2-FB04 does not restate leak_count. Scope of exposure (how many subscribers, over what period) and any statutory notification to the Bureau or to data subjects are unresolved and sit outside the NOC's authority.",
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066", "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "OpCo, as Controller, must take appropriate technical and organizational measures to protect and secure the Personal Data the chatbot can reach and to ensure it is not breached, destroyed, altered or tampered with; the cross-account disclosure means this duty is live and is not satisfied by the current retrieval configuration alone.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A chatbot session returned another subscriber's billing address and recent call records to a customer, so personal data held by OpCo left the account holder's control through an OpCo-run tool.",
        "rule": "Article 7(1) of the PDPL imposes a continuing obligation on the Controller to take technical and organisational measures to protect and secure Personal Data.",
        "inference": "The duty bites now: measures must be maintained for the chatbot's retrieval layer, not only at deployment time."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0021", "quote": "Take appropriate technical and organizational measures to implement the necessary standards to protect and secure Personal Data"}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "Before the chatbot's account-lookup processing continues, OpCo must evaluate the impact of that processing on the protection of Personal Data, as a modern technology posing a high risk to privacy and confidentiality, and must coordinate that evaluation with the Data Protection Officer.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The account-lookup tool is a modern technology that reads billing addresses and call records and has already produced a cross-subscriber disclosure.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 21 requires an impact evaluation before carrying out high-risk processing with modern technologies, and paragraph 5 requires coordination with the Data Protection Officer.",
        "inference": "The DPIA requirement is engaged by the tool itself and its evaluation must involve the DPO before the tool's processing continues."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066", "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk"},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0069", "quote": "5. The Controller shall coordinate with the Data Protection Officer upon evaluating the impact of the protection of Personal Data."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "The incident exposes the absence of a rule requiring that a customer-facing AI assistant's data retrieval be scoped to the requesting subscriber's own account, and that a completed impact evaluation be signed off before such a tool is exposed to customers.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The chatbot could return another subscriber's billing address and call records, and the fix that worked was restoring the tool with retrieval limited to the caller's own account (S2-FB04); nothing in the incident shows such scoping was ever a precondition.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 21 in substance requires a prior impact evaluation for high-risk processing with modern technologies, but no operational rule in the material scopes an assistant's retrieval or gates its go-live.",
        "inference": "A least-privilege retrieval rule and a pre-launch DPIA sign-off gate are missing from OpCo policy, which is why a customer-discoverable leak was possible."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066", "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0194", "quote": "the controller shall, prior to the processing, carry out an assessment of the impact of the envisaged processing operations on the protection of personal data"},
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0182", "quote": "a process for regularly testing, assessing and evaluating the effectiveness of technical and organisational measures for ensuring the security of the processing"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The excerpts do not say how many subscribers' records the chatbot disclosed or over what period, and they do not state whether or by when OpCo must notify the supervisory authority or the affected data subjects of this breach.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1"],
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0040", "quote": "It should be ascertained whether all appropriate technological protection and organisational measures have been implemented to establish immediately whether a personal data breach has taken place and to inform promptly the supervisory authority and the data subject."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "S2-FB04 confirms benign_success_rate returned to 1.0 after the tool was restored with own_account scope but reports no leak_count, so the feedback does not confirm that cross-account disclosure is still blocked.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-FB01", "S2-FB04"],
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0182", "quote": "a process for regularly testing, assessing and evaluating the effectiveness of technical and organisational measures for ensuring the security of the processing"}
      ]
    }
  ]}

{
  "situation": "OpCo's support chatbot exposed subscriber data: a customer screenshot shows another subscriber's billing address and recent call records, and chat-log review shows roughly 1,200 sessions over the last three days returned data from other subscribers' accounts after a vendor update changed the bot's retrieval scope from 'own account' to 'all accounts', with the vendor hosting the model and chat logs outside the UAE; the account-lookup tool was disabled and restored limited to the caller's own account and the model was rolled back, and both a TDRA licensee incident track (REG) and a PDPL Article 1 breach track (DPA Bureau) are open. We do not know the report format, scope or timeline TDRA will set, we do not hold the identity/account reference of the subscriber whose records were displayed or the full list of data subjects behind the ~1,200 sessions, and no statutory clock applies because the PDPL leaves the breach-notification period to Executive Regulations that have not been issued and the Consumer Protection Regulations v2.0 state no format or deadline for an unauthorised disclosure of Subscriber Information.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I2", "S2-MSG05", "S2-MSG09", "S2-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Log review shows about 1,200 chatbot sessions over three days returned other subscribers' data after a vendor update changed retrieval scope, the vendor hosts the chat logs outside the UAE, and the DPA Bureau requires the breach report, records of human review of the automated channel, the exact fields exposed and continued log preservation while its investigation is open.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0024 requires the Controller to maintain a special record of Personal Data covering the persons authorised to access it, processing times, limitations and scope and the security measures, and to submit it to the Bureau on request.",
        "inference": "The chatbot session records, the vendor update history, the customer's original message and the screenshot must be frozen as the evidential record supporting both the TDRA licensee report and the PDPL Article 1 report."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0024",
          "quote": "4. Maintain a special record for Personal Data, provided that such record shall include the data of both the Controller and the Data Protection Officer, a description of the categories of Personal Data, details of the persons authorized to access the Personal Data,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "REG",
        "content": "Incident scope has widened well beyond the single screenshot: chat-log review shows about 1,200 chatbot sessions over the last 3 days returned data from other subscribers' accounts, starting immediately after a vendor update changed the bot's retrieval scope from 'own account' to 'all accounts'. The vendor hosts the model and the chat logs outside the UAE. Please ensure the TDRA licensee incident record and the report scope/timeline you are setting cover all ~1,200 sessions and the offshore processing, and confirm whether TDRA requires the vendor's retrieval-scope change and the cross-border hosting to be addressed in that report. Note we are running the PDPL Article 1 notification to the Bureau as a separate track."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I2", "S2-MSG17", "S2-MSG06"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG states TDRA has opened the licensee incident record and will set report scope and timeline directly with OpCo, while the log review shows the disclosure affected ~1,200 sessions and involves processing outside the State.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0072 permits Personal Data to be transferred outside the State only in cases approved by the Bureau.",
        "inference": "REG and TDRA must be given the true scope and the cross-border element now, so the report TDRA scopes covers all affected sessions and the offshore vendor hosting rather than only the screenshot."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0072",
          "quote": "Personal Data may be transferred to outside of the State in the following cases approved by the Bureau:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "DPA",
        "content": "PDPL Article 1 breach report package for the support-chatbot cross-account disclosure, covering all ~1,200 affected sessions and all affected data subjects: (1) nature and form - subscriber account data displayed by the support chatbot to a different customer; (2) causes - a vendor update changed the bot's retrieval scope from 'own account' to 'all accounts'; (3) approximate number of records and data subjects - about 1,200 sessions over the last 3 days; (4) DPO details - to be supplied by our DPO; (5) potential and expected effects - exposure of billing address and call records to other subscribers, with the vendor hosting the model and chat logs outside the UAE; (6) corrective measures taken - account-lookup tool disabled, then restored with retrieval limited to the caller's own account, and the model rolled back to v1; (7) documents of the violation and corrective actions - preserved logs and the customer's original message and screenshot. Exact fields exposed are the billing address and recent call records; we do not yet hold the identity/account reference of the subscriber whose records were displayed and are obtaining it. Logs and evidence remain preserved pending your further requirements."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I1", "S2-I2", "S2-MSG09", "S2-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau has opened an investigation and requires OpCo's Article 1 breach report with the six listed items, records of human review of the automated channel, the identity/account reference and exact fields, and continued preservation of logs.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0024 requires the Controller to keep the special record of Personal Data, including access, scope and security measures, and to submit it to the Bureau whenever requested.",
        "inference": "The report and the underlying records must be filed with the Bureau now, on the Bureau's own timeline, because no statutory period is available to wait for."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0024",
          "quote": "The Controller shall submit such record to the Bureau whenever requested to do so."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "data_subjects",
        "content": "Notice of a personal data breach affecting your account: between the last three days, OpCo's support chatbot displayed account information - including the billing address and recent call records on your account - to a different customer. The disclosure arose from a vendor update that widened the chatbot tool's retrieval scope beyond the caller's own account. The breach prejudices the privacy and confidentiality of your personal data. Measures taken: the chatbot's account-lookup tool was switched off, then re-enabled with retrieval limited to the caller's own account, and the deployed model was rolled back to an earlier version; logs and evidence are preserved and the matter has been reported to TDRA and to the Data Protection Bureau. You have the right to request from OpCo information on the actions taken in connection with this breach or misuse, and to complain to the Bureau."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I2", "S2-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The DPA Bureau corrected its earlier position and determined that PDPL Article 1(2) requires the Controller to notify the Data Subject where the breach would prejudice the privacy and confidentiality of their personal data, informing them of the measures taken, and that the report must cover all affected data subjects and all ~1,200 sessions.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0047 gives the Data Subject the right to obtain information on the actions to be taken in the event of a breach or misuse of personal data, especially where there is a direct and serious threat to privacy and confidentiality.",
        "inference": "Affected subscribers must be told directly and now; because the notification period is left to Executive Regulations that have not been issued, OpCo must act on the regulator-set timeline rather than wait for a statutory clock."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047",
          "quote": "h. Actions to be taken in the event of a breach or misuse of his/ her Personal Data, especially if the breach or misuse has a direct and serious threat to the privacy and confidentiality of his/her Personal Data."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "File the PDPL Article 1 breach report with the Bureau covering the nature and form of the breach, its causes, the approximate number of records and data subjects (~1,200 sessions), DPO details, potential and expected effects, corrective measures taken or proposed, documents of the violation and corrective actions, anything further the Bureau requires, and the identity/account reference and exact fields exposed.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-MSG09", "S2-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau has opened an investigation into the support-chatbot disclosure and requires OpCo's Article 1 breach report, extended to all ~1,200 sessions and all affected data subjects, plus the identity/account reference and exact fields.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0024 obliges the Controller to keep the special record of Personal Data and to submit it to the Bureau whenever requested to do so.",
        "inference": "The report is owed now, covering the full session population and not only the subscriber in the screenshot; the TDRA licensee report does not discharge it."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0024",
          "quote": "The Controller shall submit such record to the Bureau whenever requested to do so."
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "Notify every data subject whose personal data was displayed by the support chatbot, across all ~1,200 affected sessions, that the breach prejudices the privacy and confidentiality of their personal data, and inform them of the measures taken.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau's corrected determination states that PDPL Article 1(2) requires the Controller in all cases to notify the Data Subject where the breach would prejudice the privacy and confidentiality of their personal data, within the period set by the Executive Regulations, and that the earlier statement denying any express proactive duty is superseded.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0047 secures the Data Subject's right to information on the actions to be taken in the event of a breach or misuse of their personal data, especially where there is a direct and serious threat.",
        "inference": "A proactive notification duty to data subjects now applies and cannot be left to a regime that has not been issued; since no hour or day figure is stated in any instrument, no deadline figure can be quoted."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047",
          "quote": "h. Actions to be taken in the event of a breach or misuse of his/ her Personal Data, especially if the breach or misuse has a direct and serious threat to the privacy and confidentiality of his/her Personal Data."
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "No statutory clock governs either the PDPL breach notification to the Bureau and to data subjects or the reporting of an unauthorised disclosure of Subscriber Information: the PDPL leaves the period to Executive Regulations that have not been issued, and the Consumer Protection Regulations v2.0 state no format or deadline, so both obligations run only to a timeline a regulator sets case by case after the incident.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-MSG06", "S2-MSG12", "S2-MSG15", "S2-MSG17"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG and the DPA both state that no hour or day deadline can be quoted: the PDPL refers the breach-notification period to Executive Regulations not yet issued and the Consumer Protection Regulations v2.0 state no format or deadline for unauthorised disclosure of Subscriber Information, with TDRA setting scope and timeline directly with OpCo.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0047 describes actions to be taken in the event of a breach or misuse of personal data but attaches no time limit to them.",
        "inference": "Because no fixed period exists in UAE text, OpCo's notification speed depends on regulator discretion rather than a defined duty, which is the gap the incident exposes."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047",
          "quote": "h. Actions to be taken in the event of a breach or misuse of his/ her Personal Data, especially if the breach or misuse has a direct and serious threat to the privacy and confidentiality of his/her Personal Data."
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0040",
          "quote": "(87) It should be ascertained whether all appropriate technological protection and organisational measures have been implemented to establish immediately whether a personal data breach has taken place and to inform promptly the supervisory authority and the data subject."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "There is no rule requiring pre-deployment assessment or change control when a vendor update changes the behaviour of an automated customer-service channel: the bot's retrieval scope moved from 'own account' to 'all accounts' with no approval or testing step, even though Article 21 requires a data protection impact assessment before using high-risk modern technologies, and nothing requires that assessment to be revisited when a live model or its retrieval scope is changed.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I2", "S2-FB01", "S2-FB04", "S2-FB10"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "About 1,200 sessions over three days returned other subscribers' accounts starting right after a vendor update changed the bot's retrieval scope from 'own account' to 'all accounts', and the twin only recovered benign_success_rate after the scope was restored to own_account and the model was rolled back to v1.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0066 requires the Controller to evaluate, before carrying out the processing, the impact of proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data when using modern technologies posing a high risk to privacy and confidentiality.",
        "inference": "The DPIA duty exists for the initial deployment but the incident shows no rule or control point forcing re-assessment and approval of vendor changes to a deployed model, which is the gap that let the scope change go live unchecked."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies"
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0002",
          "quote": "insufficient safeguards and controls to ensure approval of changes affecting the core network"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "The incident exposes the absence of a rule governing AI/chatbot vendors that host models and interaction logs outside the UAE: Article 22 allows transfer outside the State only in cases approved by the Bureau, but nothing specifies how an offshore-hosted model and its chat logs are to be assessed or approved before subscriber data is passed through them.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I2", "S2-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The vendor hosts the model and the chat logs outside the UAE, and those chat logs are the record of ~1,200 cross-account disclosures involving subscriber billing address and call records.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0072 permits Personal Data to be transferred outside the State only in the cases approved by the Bureau.",
        "inference": "Because no specific rule ties offshore model and log hosting to a pre-approved transfer basis in an automated customer-service deployment, OpCo cannot show a clear compliance route for the processing that caused the incident."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0072",
          "quote": "Personal Data may be transferred to outside of the State in the following cases approved by the Bureau:"
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0052",
          "quote": "When personal data moves across borders outside the Union it may put at increased risk the ability of natural persons to exercise data protection rights in particular to protect themselves from the unlawful use or disclosure of that information."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "overlap",
      "statement": "The same disclosure is being reported on two separate tracks - the TDRA licensee incident record with its own TDRA-set scope and timeline, and the PDPL Article 1 breach notification to the Bureau with its own Bureau-set requirements - with no designated single owner or harmonised format, content and deadline for the two filings.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-MSG06", "S2-MSG09", "S2-MSG15", "S2-MSG17"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG reports that TDRA has opened a licensee incident record and will set report scope and timeline directly with OpCo, while the DPA Bureau has opened an investigation and requires an Article 1 report with the six listed items, expressly stating that TDRA licensee reporting is a separate track that does not satisfy the PDPL notification.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0032 provides for the record to be submitted to the Bureau whenever it is requested, giving one submission channel, but no UAE text designates a single coordinating owner across the licensee and data-protection tracks.",
        "inference": "Two regulators can demand overlapping reports on the same facts with different content and timing, so OpCo risks inconsistent filings and duplicated effort unless one function owns and reconciles both."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0032",
          "quote": "provided that the Processor submits such record to the Bureau whenever it is requested to do so"
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0040",
          "quote": "In setting detailed rules concerning the format and procedures applicable to the notification of personal data breaches, due consideration should be given to the circumstances of that breach"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I2"],
      "statement": "Passing the log-review item to REG and the DPA Bureau: about 1,200 chatbot sessions over the last 3 days returned data from other subscribers' accounts, starting right after a vendor update changed the bot's retrieval scope from 'own account' to 'all accounts', and the vendor hosts the model and the chat logs outside the UAE. This defines the true population for both the TDRA licensee report and the PDPL Article 1 report, not only the single subscriber in the screenshot."
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The excerpts do not answer which subscriber's records were displayed (the identity/account reference the Bureau requires), do not provide the complete list of data subjects behind the ~1,200 sessions, and do not provide records of human review of the automated channel; OpCo holds no dated obligation to work to, because no UAE text states an hour or day figure for the PDPL or Consumer Protection Regulations reporting duties.",
      "inject_refs": ["S2-MSG09", "S2-MSG15", "S2-MSG17"],
      "evidence": []
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "OpCo's support chatbot returned other subscribers' billing addresses and call records across about 1,200 sessions over three days, beginning right after a vendor update changed the bot's retrieval scope from 'own account' to 'all accounts', with the vendor hosting the model and chat logs outside the UAE; a prompt-injection technique targeting telecom support chatbots is being sold on a criminal forum, TDRA has opened a licensee incident record covering the full scope, DPA has filed a full-scope PDPL Article 1 notification, a data subject has complained directly, and containment (lookup disabled then limited to the caller's own account, model rolled back to v1, human review queue enabled) is reported done. I do not have the affected subscriber's identity/account reference, the exact per-subscriber field list, the number of distinct affected data subjects, the human-review records or DPO details, TDRA's report format and timeline, or any CPR v2.0 reporting format or deadline for an unauthorised disclosure of Subscriber Information.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "issue_advisory",
      "params": {
        "audience": "operators",
        "content": "CSC operator advisory (update): a prompt-injection technique that causes telecom support chatbots to return other subscribers' records is being sold on a criminal forum. Operators should (1) constrain chatbot/assistant retrieval to the authenticated caller's own account; (2) require change-review and notification for vendor-initiated retrieval-scope, model or configuration changes, and treat offshore hosting of models and chat logs as in-scope; (3) monitor, log and alert on cross-account retrieval in chatbot sessions; and (4) route borderline automated refusals to a human review queue, because restricting retrieval alone stops the leaks but breaks legitimate requests. Preserve logs and report incidents; share indicators with CSC."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S2-I3", "S2-MSG22", "S2-FB01", "S2-FB24"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Threat intel (S2-I3) says a prompt-injection technique making telecom support chatbots return other customers' records is sold on a criminal forum; twin feedback shows disabling/limiting lookup drives leak_count to 0 but benign_success_rate to 0.2, while an added human review queue restores legit_pass_rate to 1.0 without locking out banking.",
        "rule": "The National Cyber Security Policy for AI requires understanding and anticipating adversarial AI attacks, with continuous monitoring, testing and robust incident response; CSC issues advisories to protect against threats.",
        "inference": "CSC should push an updated operator advisory combining the new TTP with the control set validated in the twin (own-account retrieval scope plus human review queue)."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-CSC-AI-POLICY#0001",
          "quote": "Requirements to understand and anticipate potential AI/ML attacks, fostering a proactive security approach that includes education, awareness, and comprehensive defense techniques, continuous monitoring, testing, and robust incident response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
